---
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';

const siteUrl = 'https://shadowharness.github.io/';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const pageUrl = `${siteUrl}en/security/`;
const title = 'Data Handling & Trust — ShadowHarness';
const description =
	'How Sidera Memory handles terminal logs across your device, Shadow Hub, the LLM used to structure memory, and each CLI provider.';
---

<SiteLayout
	lang="en"
	title={title}
	description={description}
	pageUrl={pageUrl}
	alternateJa={`${siteUrl}security/`}
	alternateEn={pageUrl}
>
	<main id="main-content" class="guide-page" tabindex="-1">
		<header class="guide-hero">
			<p class="eyebrow">DATA HANDLING &amp; TRUST</p>
			<h1>Data Handling &amp; Trust</h1>
			<p class="lead">
				Sidera Memory sends terminal conversation logs to Shadow Hub and uses LLM judgment
				to organize them into memory the next task can use.
			</p>
			<p>
				This page makes clear what leaves your device, where an LLM is involved, and where
				ShadowHarness ends and each AI provider's settings begin.
			</p>
		</header>

		<div class="guide-layout">
			<aside class="guide-nav">
				<p>On this page</p>
				<nav aria-label="Data handling contents">
					<a href="#summary">What to know first</a>
					<a href="#flow">Sidera data flow</a>
					<a href="#training">Structuring and training</a>
					<a href="#providers">The provider boundary</a>
					<a href="#storage">What lives where</a>
					<a href="#risk">Risk is not zero</a>
					<a href="#updates">Updates and contact</a>
				</nav>
			</aside>

			<article class="guide-content prose">
				<section id="summary">
					<p class="eyebrow">01 / SUMMARY</p>
					<h2>What to know first</h2>
					<div class="trust-grid">
						<div>
							<h3>Logs go to the Hub</h3>
							<p>
								When you use Sidera Memory, new ranges of the terminal logs left by each
								CLI on your PC are sent to Shadow Hub.
							</p>
						</div>
						<div>
							<h3>LLM judgment is involved</h3>
							<p>
								An LLM helps identify the current conclusion, the history of changes, and
								the relationship between a conclusion and its evidence.
							</p>
						</div>
						<div>
							<h3>Structuring input is not used for training</h3>
							<p>
								Input sent for Sidera's structuring is processed under settings that do
								not allow it to be used for model training.
							</p>
						</div>
						<div>
							<h3>Each CLI is a separate boundary</h3>
							<p>
								Data use by Codex, Claude Code, Grok, Gemini, and other providers follows
								their terms and your settings with them.
							</p>
						</div>
					</div>
				</section>

				<section id="flow">
					<p class="eyebrow">02 / DATA FLOW</p>
					<h2>How data moves through Sidera Memory</h2>
					<ol>
						<li>
							<strong>Each CLI writes its raw log on your PC.</strong>{' '}
							The CLI determines the location and format.
						</li>
						<li>
							<strong>ShadowHarness detects new log ranges.</strong>{' '}
							Terminal log data needed by Sidera Memory is sent to Shadow Hub.
						</li>
						<li>
							<strong>The Hub structures it into memory.</strong>{' '}
							Alongside mechanical indexing, LLM judgment helps connect conclusions,
							changes, and supporting evidence.
						</li>
						<li>
							<strong>The relevant part is returned for the current input.</strong>{' '}
							The working AI pane receives memory selected for this task, not the entire history.
						</li>
					</ol>
					<div class="guide-callout">
						A terminal log can contain more than what you typed. It may include command
						output and other content the AI handled during the conversation. Do not assume
						that only your question is sent.
					</div>
				</section>

				<section id="training">
					<p class="eyebrow">03 / LLM PROCESSING</p>
					<h2>Structuring memory and model training</h2>
					<p>
						Sidera Memory cannot be built with keyword search alone. It uses LLM judgment to
						determine which conclusion is current, when an earlier decision changed, and
						which record supports it.
					</p>
					<p>
						Input sent for this structuring is handled under settings that do not allow it
						to be used for model training. This does not mean that no data reaches an LLM:
						the structuring input is processed by an LLM, but under conditions that exclude
						that input from training data.
					</p>
					<div class="guide-callout">
						This commitment covers the processing ShadowHarness performs to structure
						Sidera memory. It does not override how the AI service behind each CLI handles data.
					</div>
				</section>

				<section id="providers">
					<p class="eyebrow">04 / PROVIDER BOUNDARY</p>
					<h2>Codex, Claude Code, Grok, Gemini, and others remain separate services</h2>
					<p>
						Input you send to an AI agent, and the response it generates, are also processed
						by that provider's service. Retention, use for service improvement or training,
						and organization-level protections depend on your plan and settings with that provider.
					</p>
					<p>
						ShadowHarness's Sidera settings do not change those provider settings. Conversely,
						even if training use is disabled with a provider, using Sidera Memory still sends
						the terminal log data needed to build memory to Shadow Hub. Evaluate the two
						boundaries separately.
					</p>
				</section>

				<section id="storage">
					<p class="eyebrow">05 / STORAGE</p>
					<h2>What lives on your device and what lives on Shadow Hub</h2>
					<dl class="facts-list">
						<div>
							<dt>Your PC</dt>
							<dd>
								Each CLI's raw log remains in the location that CLI chooses. The
								ShadowHarness client also keeps local state needed to track
								collection and sending progress.
							</dd>
						</div>
						<div>
							<dt>Shadow Hub</dt>
							<dd>
								The Hub receives log data sent for Sidera Memory and holds the structured
								memory and indexes built from it.
							</dd>
						</div>
						<div>
							<dt>The working AI pane</dt>
							<dd>
								Memory judged relevant to the current input is supplied as context in the
								range needed to continue the work.
							</dd>
						</div>
					</dl>
					<p>
						Raw logs remain on the device, while data used to maintain memory also lives on
						the Hub. Neither side is the sole location for every part of the system.
					</p>
				</section>

				<section id="risk">
					<p class="eyebrow">06 / RISK</p>
					<h2>Risk is not zero</h2>
					<p>
						Sending terminal logs over a network and processing them through the Hub and an
						LLM carries non-zero risks, including unauthorized disclosure, incorrect access,
						and service failure. These are the same kinds of risk involved when sending work
						to a general cloud LLM.
					</p>
					<p>
						A terminal conversation can contain source code, file paths, working decisions,
						and command output. Consider the sensitivity of the work and review the settings
						for both each AI provider and Sidera Memory before using them.
					</p>
					<p>
						ShadowHarness reduces risk by making the data path explicit and by processing
						structuring input under settings that exclude it from training. We do not turn
						those measures into a promise that disclosure is impossible.
					</p>
				</section>

				<section id="updates">
					<p class="eyebrow">07 / UPDATES</p>
					<h2>When data handling changes</h2>
					<p>
						If the Sidera Memory processing path or data boundary changes, this page will be
						updated as well. Development updates and official notices are available from{' '}
						<a href="https://x.com/ShadowHarness" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
							@ShadowHarness
						</a>.
					</p>
					<p><a href={`${base}en/guide/`}>Return to the ShadowHarness Guide</a></p>
				</section>
			</article>
		</div>
	</main>
</SiteLayout>
